{"report_id":"R0074","domain":"D06","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Two separate Monte Carlo experiments are reported: an averaged multi-run break-even microbenchmark for predictive decoding in §4.7 (p20) and multi-trial noise-injection robustness experiments with latency distribution, coefficient of variation, and CDF dispersion reporting in §4.8 (p21). However, neither replication counts nor dispersion measures carry over to the main baseline comparisons in §4.2 (p16) or §§4.3-4.6, which report single point latency values without error bars or repeat counts.","locators":[{"page":20,"section":"4.7. Investigation of Prediction Accuracy on End-to-End Latency","quote":"we performed 300 Monte Carlo simulations for each configuration and took the average values as the final results."},{"page":21,"section":"4.8. Robustness Analysis Under Environmental Uncertainty","quote":"independent trials executed for each"},{"page":21,"section":"4.8. Robustness Analysis Under Environmental Uncertainty","quote":"the nominal latency curve closely adheres to the clean baseline"},{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales, inspected replication and dispersion reporting","quote":null}]}
{"report_id":"R0074","domain":"D08","judgment":"NO","rationale":"The Introduction (p3) claims Orion is the only framework capable of running the full 70B model on memory-constrained UAV nodes, which is directly contradicted by §4.2 (pp16-17): under the shared simulated comparison settings, Edge-Solo and the cloud-edge baselines fail with out-of-memory errors, but distributed baselines EdgeShard and Jupiter also successfully execute the 70B model alongside Orion. In addition, §5.5 (p23) confirms that all results come from simulation rather than a physical UAV hardware testbed, bounding conclusions away from hardware trials.","locators":[{"page":3,"section":"1. Introduction","quote":"being the only framework capable of running the full 70B model on memory-constrained UAV nodes"},{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"we fixed the sensor input length to 64 tokens to evaluate the scalability of Orion across different model sizes."},{"page":17,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"Among all successful distributed solutions, Orion remained the most efficient"},{"page":23,"section":"5.5. Real-System Deployment and Physical Validation","quote":"We plan to implement Orion on a physical UAV testbed"}]}
{"report_id":"R0083","domain":"D04","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The proposed method is compared against NN, HRE, and LO under the same simulated swarm setup, deployment areas, and battery configurations (§4.2, p7). Comparability is limited because HRE is a battery-modified variant of a prior memory-based heuristic, LO ignores battery capacity, and NN effectively used only a single device because model memory did not exceed single-device capacity under the tested memory settings (§4.2, p8), reflecting policy behavior under those settings rather than an externally imposed resource disparity.","locators":[{"page":7,"section":"4.2. Simulation results","quote":"compare our method with results from nearest neighbor (NN), highest remaining energy (HRE) and latency only (LO)."},{"page":8,"section":"4.2. Simulation results","quote":"in the tested memory settings, the memory of the models did not exceed the device capacity"}]}
{"report_id":"R0089","domain":"D03","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Section 5 (p8) discloses core simulation settings including edge/drone counts, compute capacities, task generation model, latency bounds, transmit power, path loss factor, frequency band, cycle count, PyTorch, and CIFAR-10, and §5.1 (p9) specifies that wireless resources scale proportionally as the percentage of raw data each drone can upload. However, material rerun details remain unspecified in §5 (p8) and §5.1 (p9), including absolute channel bandwidth, noise power spectral density, inter-node distances/channel gains, intermediate bottleneck data sizes, random seeds, and numeric values for the heuristic latency and early-exit entropy thresholds.","locators":[{"page":8,"section":"5. Simulation and numerical results","quote":"The simulation scenario includes 4 edge servers with 12 drone terminals."},{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Available radio resource","quote":"Wireless resources increase proportionally to the overall task number, corresponding to the percentage of raw data that each drone terminal can upload."},{"page":8,"section":"5. Simulation and numerical results, inspected absolute bandwidth, noise spectral density, channel distances, intermediate data sizes, and heuristic threshold values","quote":null},{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Available radio resource, inspected absolute channel bandwidth and noise power spectral density values","quote":null}]}
{"report_id":"R0095","domain":"D05","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Section 5.3 (p9) defines cumulative inference latency (aggregating computational, communication, and model-loading delays), task completion rate, cache-driven latency comparison, and reward convergence, aligning with the formulated latency-minimization objective, while §5.4.2 (p10, Fig. 9) separately reports average single-decision time as algorithmic overhead. However, §5.1 (p8) explicitly excludes the core UAV scheduling and policy-inference overhead from task inference latency, so measured decision overhead is not folded into the inference-latency components, and task-level DNN inference accuracy is not evaluated.","locators":[{"page":8,"section":"5.1. Experimental environment and parameter settings","quote":"its computation overhead is not included in the task inference latency"},{"page":9,"section":"5.3. Performance indicators","quote":"aggregating computational, communication, and model-loading delays"},{"page":10,"section":"5.4.2. Comparison of partitioning schemes","quote":"we compare the average decision latency of different dynamic partitioning algorithms"}]}
{"report_id":"R0121","domain":"D02","judgment":"YES","rationale":"Model variables, payload/header definitions, and parameter values are explicitly defined in §§5-6 (pp5-6), including the requirement that a tensor is usable only when complete and the MSDU versus Ethernet MTU framing assumptions. Section 7 (pp8-9) transparently states the simplifying analytical assumptions—averaged medium access costs omitting high-density collisions, independent frame-delivery probabilities omitting correlated freezes and jamming, omission of 802.11 frame aggregation, and single-hop numerical scope—clearly distinguishing these simplified model assumptions from observed collision-free network operations.","locators":[{"page":5,"section":"5. An Analytical Model of Delay and Traffic Volume","quote":"Since the tensor is only useful to the aggregator when complete"},{"page":6,"section":"6. Numerical Results and Their Discussion","quote":"The model parameters are given in Table 2"},{"page":8,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"uses averaged medium access costs and does not account for collisions at high swarm densities"},{"page":9,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"the numerical estimates presented in this work were obtained for a single hop"}]}
{"report_id":"R0121","domain":"D03","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The analytical equations in §5 (pp5-6) and the parameter table in §6 (Table 2, p6) provide the core inputs needed to reconstruct the analytical model and its primary estimates. Reproducibility is partially limited by an ambiguity between the §6 paragraph discussing Fig. 3 (p7) and the Conclusions (p9) regarding the quorum comparison conditions underlying the reported quorum probabilities, as well as the absence of calculation script details, though this textual ambiguity does not prevent reconstructing the analytical model itself.","locators":[{"page":6,"section":"6. Numerical Results and Their Discussion","quote":"The model parameters are given in Table 2"},{"page":7,"section":"6. Numerical Results and Their Discussion","quote":"This difference directly translates into the probability of achieving a quorum"},{"page":9,"section":"Conclusions","quote":"which raises the probability of a collective decision quorum"}]}
{"report_id":"R0121","domain":"D05","judgment":"YES","rationale":"The reported numerical outcomes in §§5-6 (pp5-8) directly match the article's stated analytical objectives in §3 (p3) to model and compare inter-node traffic volume, transmission time, and aggregation quorum probability between L2 encapsulation and the L2-L7 stack across quantization schemes. Degraded-mode inference accuracy is not measured and is explicitly deferred to future experiments in §7 (p8), representing an additional unmeasured outcome distinct from the paper's stated analytical objectives rather than a claimed measured result.","locators":[{"page":3,"section":"3. Purpose and Problem Statement","quote":"Build an analytical model of traffic volume, transmission time, and quorum probability."},{"page":6,"section":"6. Numerical Results and Their Discussion","quote":"The calculations were performed for a reference feature tensor"},{"page":8,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"the model does not evaluate the accuracy of inference under degraded conditions"}]}
{"report_id":"R0121","domain":"D07","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The evaluation is explicitly scoped as analytical, single-hop numerical calculations built on simplified model assumptions rather than simulation or field experiments (§7, pp8-9). Averaged medium-access costs, omission of high-density collisions, independent frame-delivery probabilities without correlated freezes or jamming, omission of 802.11 frame aggregation, and single-hop calculations are declared model abstractions rather than observed collision-free behavior or tested mobility, limiting realism relative to mobile multi-hop FANET swarms under electronic countermeasures.","locators":[{"page":8,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"uses averaged medium access costs and does not account for collisions at high swarm densities"},{"page":8,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"assumed to be independent, which does not account for correlated freezes and intentional jamming"},{"page":9,"section":"7. Limitations of the research","quote":"the numerical estimates presented in this work were obtained for a single hop"}]}
